Welcome to the AutoAnki repository!

The purpose of this project is to automate the creation of Anki cards using the OpenAI API, as well as the
genanki python package.

Here's what everything does:

input/words: Contains input used by the command-line generation script.

input/prompts/: Contains every prompt discovered automatically and shown in
the Card setup tab. Built-in detailed and simple prompts are provided for
English, Classical Chinese, French, Japanese, and Latin.

src/process_text.py: Calls the OpenAI API and converts JSON responses into
genanki packages. API responses use a strict JSON schema and are validated
again locally before any package is written.

src/templates.py: Contains the registered genanki card types. English supports
context-sentence, word-to-definition, and definition-to-word cards. Classical
Chinese, French, and Japanese provide those same three directions with either
an English definition or a definition written in the language itself. Latin
provides the same six outputs.

output/: Stores generated responses and packages at output/response.json and
output/output.apkg.

Running the user interface:

1. From the repository root, run:

   .venv/bin/python src/gui.py

2. Enter words or notes in the scrolling text box and click
   "Generate and import".

The interface uses screen-aware sizing and DPI scaling, rounded card surfaces
and controls, a non-tiled scrollbar, transient autosave feedback, and explicit
busy/progress states. Clicking a non-interactive background clears transient
focus and text highlighting. AutoAnki prevents the window from closing while
a paid response is still being generated or imported.

Configuring card generation:

Open the Card setup tab. Select:

1. The language tab matching the input.
2. One or more card outputs inside that language tab.
3. That language's target deck from inside its tab, or enable separate target
   decks and choose one beside each selected card output.

Each language tab retains its own card selections, shared target deck,
separate-deck mode, and per-card target decks. Switching languages does not
replace the preferences saved for the other language tabs.

AutoAnki chooses the cheapest sufficient prompt automatically. If only simple
word/definition outputs are selected, the response asks only for the word and
the selected English and/or native-language definitions. If any
context-sentence output is selected, the richer prompt adds examples and
pronunciation once. The same response is reused for every selected card output,
so a generation run makes exactly one API request regardless of how many
outputs are selected.

Opening Card setup or a target-deck selector connects to Anki, waits for its
collection to become available, and refreshes the choices. The most recently
seen deck list is cached for the next launch, and the editable selectors still
allow an exact deck name to be entered if Anki is unavailable. Every setting
is saved automatically in the current user's AutoAnki configuration directory.

Editing prompts:

The Advanced tab lists every file discovered in input/prompts. Prompts open
in read-only mode. Tick "Enable prompt editing" to unlock the text editor,
then use "Save prompt" to apply an atomic update. Empty prompts and paths
outside input/prompts are rejected. AutoAnki asks whether to save, discard, or
cancel if unsaved changes exist while switching prompts, disabling editing,
or closing the application.

genanki identities:

Every card type has a unique model ID generated once and hard-coded in
src/templates.py. The generated temporary deck also retains its stable deck
ID, and generated notes use stable GUIDs. All generated note types share a
22px `.card` styling rule while retaining their individual front/back HTML
and JavaScript.

On the first generation, AutoAnki asks for an OpenAI API key. The key is
entered through a masked field and stored outside the repository in the
current user's configuration directory. On Linux, the default location is
~/.config/autoanki/openai_api_key. The directory and file use owner-only
permissions. Use the "API key" menu to replace or forget the saved key.

OPENAI_API_KEY can still be set as an environment variable and takes
precedence over the saved key.

Opening the interface does not call the OpenAI API. Requests are made only
after "Generate and import" is clicked.

If generation fails or OpenAI returns an incomplete/refused response, the GUI
shows the failure and does not retry automatically. A new paid request is made
only if the user explicitly chooses Retry; automatic retries in the OpenAI
client are disabled. Anki import failures retain the generated package and do
not prompt for a paid regeneration.

Automatic Anki import:

AutoAnki uses the local AnkiConnect API and does not control the mouse or
keyboard. Install AnkiConnect once in Anki:

1. Open Tools > Add-ons > Get Add-ons.
2. Enter the add-on code 2055492159.
3. Restart Anki.

After generating a package, AutoAnki:

1. Starts Anki if AnkiConnect is not already available.
   On Linux/X11, a newly launched Anki window is minimized automatically
   without controlling the mouse or keyboard.
2. Waits for Anki, the active profile, and any automatic startup sync to
   finish.
3. Imports each generated package.
4. Moves every package's cards into the selected target deck, or routes each
   selected card type to its separate target deck when that option is enabled.
5. Verifies each temporary deck is empty, then deletes it.

Every selected target deck must already exist.
If Anki is installed under a non-standard command, set
AUTOANKI_ANKI_COMMAND to the command used to start it. An optional AnkiConnect
API key can be supplied through ANKICONNECT_API_KEY. AutoAnki does not trigger
synchronization itself; Anki's automatic synchronization settings remain in
control.
